int of \\(S\\), then the other five points would have to be covered by the two sunny lines.\nEach line can contain at most three points of \\(S\\) (because any three points of \\(S\\) are never collinear unless they lie on a non\u2011sunny line, see the enumeration below).  \nThus two lines could cover at most \\(2\\cdot 3=6\\) points, but to cover five points while avoiding the single point on \\(L\\) each line would need to contain at least three points of \\(S\\setminus\\{L\\}\\), which is impossible because no three points of \\(S\\) are collinear on a sunny line (they are either vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\)).  \nHence \\(L\\) must contain **at least two** points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  All non\u2011sunny lines containing at least two points of \\(S\\)\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions:\n* vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)),\n* horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)),\n* slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\nScanning the six points we obtain the following lines of these three families that contain at least two points of \\(S\\):\n\n| Direction | Equation | Points of \\(S\\) on the line |\n|-----------|----------|-----------------------------|\n| vertical  | \\(x=1\\)  | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) |\n| vertical  | \\(x=2\\)  | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) |\n| horizontal| \\(y=1\\)  | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) |\n| horizontal| \\(y=2\\)  | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\) |\n\nNo other line of the three allowed directions passes through two points of \\(S\\).\n\nThus the possible choices for the non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) are exactly the six lines listed above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The complement of each candidate line cannot be covered by two sunny lines\n\nFor each candidate \\(L\\) we list the complement \\(S\\setminus L\\) and compute the slopes of all lines determined by any two points of this complement.  \nIn every case the slope is either \\(0\\) (horizontal), undefined (vertical), or \\(-1\\); consequently every such line is non\u2011sunny.\n\n| \\(L\\) | \\(S\\setminus L\\) | Slopes of all pairs in the complement |\n|------|------------------|----------------------------------------|\n| \\(x=1\\) | \\((2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\) | \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-1}{2-3}=0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-2}{2-2}\\) undefined (vertical), \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-2}{2-3}=1\\) (slope \\(1\\) \u2013 **sunny**). However the remaining two points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) are already on a horizontal line, which is non\u2011sunny, and any line covering the third point together with one of the first two is either vertical or has slope \\(1\\). To cover all three points with **two** sunny lines we would need a sunny line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), which does not exist. Hence impossible. |\n| \\(x=2\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\) | Pairs involving the three points with \\(x=1\\) give vertical lines; pairs \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) have slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (sunny), but then \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) remain and are vertical (non\u2011sunny). Thus two sunny lines cannot cover all four points. |\n| \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | Pairs \\((1,2),(1,3)\\) vertical, \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) horizontal, \\((1,3),(3,1)\\) slope \\(-1\\); any line through two of the remaining points is non\u2011sunny. |\n| \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1)\\) | All pairs are either horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\). |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\) | Pairs \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) slope \\(1\\) (sunny), but the other two points \\((1,3),(3,1)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\) (non\u2011sunny). Hence at least one of the two required sunny lines would have to be non\u2011sunny. |\n| \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) | Every pair among these three points yields a vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\) line \u2013 all non\u2011sunny. |\n\nIn each case the set \\(S\\setminus L\\) cannot be partitioned into at most two sunny lines. Therefore no choice of a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) allows the remaining two lines to be sunny and still cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nAssuming the existence of a configuration with exactly two sunny lines leads to a contradiction in every possible placement of the unique non\u2011sunny line. Consequently such a configuration does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with exactly }k=2\\text{ sunny lines is impossible.}}\n\\]"
