\(-2\\)        |\n| \\(x+y=3\\)             | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\\) | only \\((1,1)-(2,2)\\) with slope \\(1\\)         |\n| \\(x+y=4\\)             | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\)   | none (slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\))                |\n\n*If the complement contains no sunny pair* (first and last rows), any sunny line can contain at most one point of the complement; therefore at least three sunny lines would be required, contradicting the assumption that exactly two sunny lines are available.\n\n*If the complement contains exactly one sunny pair* (rows 2–5), a sunny line can cover that pair, leaving two points that lie on a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (hence non‑sunny). The remaining sunny line cannot contain both of those points, so again we cannot cover the complement with only two sunny lines.\n\nThus for **every** possible choice of the non‑sunny line \\(L\\) the set \\(S\\setminus L\\) cannot be covered by the two required sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Contradiction and conclusion\n\nOur assumption that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists leads to a contradiction in every possible placement of the unique non‑sunny line. Hence such a configuration does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with exactly }k=2\\text{ sunny lines is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
