e distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n   - **Verification of coverage.**  \n     Let a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b>n-2k\\).  Write  \n\n     \\[\n     b=n-2k+t\\qquad\\text{with }t\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,2k\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a\\le 2k+1-t\\).  Hence the set of admissible points in this row is  \n\n     \\[\n     \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le 2k+1-t\\}.\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n     For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     a_i = i-\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{t}{2}\\bigr\\rceil .\n     \\]\n\n     One checks that \\(a_i\\) is an integer satisfying \\(1\\le a_i\\le 2k+1-t\\) **iff** \\(i\\ge\\lceil t/2\\rceil\\).  \n     Consequently the points  \n\n     \\[\n     (a_i ,\\,b)\\qquad\\bigl(i=\\lceil t/2\\rceil,\\dots ,k\\bigr)\n     \\]\n\n     are exactly the points of row \\(b\\) that lie on the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) (because on \\(L_i\\) we have \\(b=a+(n-2k+i-1)\\), i.e. \\(a=b-(n-2k+i-1)=i-\\lceil t/2\\rceil\\)).  \n     The number of such points is \\(k-\\lceil t/2\\rceil+1 = 2k+1-t\\), which coincides with the number of points required by (1).  Hence **every** point of the row \\(b\\) is covered by one of the lines \\(L_{\\lceil t/2\\rceil},L_{\\lceil t/2\\rceil+1},\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n     Combining the two parts we see that every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n   - **Counting sunny lines.**  The construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\), all sunny, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are horizontal, hence non‑sunny.  Thus the configuration satisfies the required condition.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); Lemma 2 shows that this bound is also sufficient.  Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those described in the boxed answer.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathb