            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) consider the horizontal row  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(R_b\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|R_b|=n+1-b .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line that is not horizontal meets a given row in **at most one** point, because two distinct points of the same row would have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate and therefore the line would be horizontal.  \nConsequently every *sunny* line contributes at most one point to each row.\n\nLet the rows with the largest \\(b\\) be those with \\(b=n,n-1,\\dots ,n-m+1\\).  \nUsing (1) the total number of points that lie in these \\(m\\) rows equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}(j+1)=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf we have \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of them can cover at most one point in each of the \\(m\\) rows, so the sunny lines together can cover at most \\(km\\) points of the set (2).  \nHence we must have  \n\n\\[\nkm\\ge\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\ge\\frac{m+1}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nChoose \\(m=2k\\).  Then (3) becomes \\(k\\ge k+\\tfrac12\\), which is impossible.  \nTherefore we must have \\(2k\\le n-1\\); equivalently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\; } .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nm=n-2k\\qquad(\\text{note that }m\\ge 1).\n\\]\n\n**(i) Non‑sunny lines.**  \nTake the \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and the lines are distinct.\n\n**(ii) Sunny lines.**  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=x+(m+i-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); they are therefore sunny (they are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\) which has slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThe intercepts \\(m+i-1\\) are distinct, so the \\(L_i\\) are distinct.\n\nWe have produced exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, with precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le m\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) from (5).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b>m\\).*  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nb=m+t\\qquad\\text{with }t\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,2k\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b=n+1-(m+t)=2k+1-t .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the admissible points in this row are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,m+t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le 2k+1-t\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(\\lceil t/2\\rceil\\le i\\le k\\) consider the line \\(L_i\\) from (6).  \nOn \\(L_i\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ny = x + (m+i-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nx = y-(m+i-1).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(y=m+t\\) gives the point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(t-i+1,\\;m+t\\bigr).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\ge\\lceil t/2\\rceil\\), the first coordinate in (10) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n1\\le t-i+1\\le 2k+1-t,\n\\]\n\nthe lower inequality follows from \\(i\\le t\\) (which holds for all \\(i\\) in the stated range), and the upper inequality is a direct rearrangement of \\(i\\ge\\lceil t/2\\rceil\\).  \nHence the point (10) belongs to the set (9).  \n\nConversely, every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le 2k+1-t\\) can be written uniquely as \\(a=t-i+1\\) with \\(i=t-a+1\\); the latter satisfies \\(\\lceil t/2\\rceil\\le i\\le k\\).  Therefore each point of (9) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_{\\lceil t/2\\rceil},L_{\\lceil t/2\\rceil+1},\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\nConsequently **every** point with \\(b>m\\) is covered by one of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\).\n\nSince the two cases exhaust all points of \\(S_n\\), the family of lines (5)–(6) indeed covers the whole set.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\n- Inequality (4) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n- Lemma 2 (the construction above) shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n  \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\; } .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n#