uivalent to \\(i\\ge\\lceil t/2\\rceil\\).  \n   Consequently each integer \\(a\\) satisfying (6) appears exactly once as \\(a=t-i+1\\) for a suitable \\(i\\) with \\(i_0\\le i\\le k\\).  \n   Hence the whole set (7) is covered by the sunny lines \\(L_{i_0},L_{i_0+1},\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   In both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (4)–(5).  \n   The family consists of \\(m\\) horizontal lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(n=m+k\\) distinct lines, and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) a required configuration exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (3) shows that no configuration can contain more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, and the explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   can indeed be realised.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the set displayed in the boxed answer. ∎"
