            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\, } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   Consider the two families of “extreme’’ rows  \n   \\[\n   R_{n-2k+1},R_{n-2k+2},\\dots ,R_{n},\n   \\qquad\\text{(there are }2k\\text{ rows)} .\n   \\]\n   In each of these rows the number of points of \\(S_n\\) is\n   \\[\n   |R_{n-2k+t}|=2k+1-t\\qquad(t=1,\\dots ,2k).\n   \\]\n   Hence the total number of points that lie in the \\(2k\\) rows equals  \n\n   \\[\n   P_k=\\sum_{t=1}^{2k}(2k+1-t)=k(2k+1). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   A line that is **not** horizontal meets each of the \\(2k\\) rows in at most one point; a horizontal line can meet at most one of those rows (otherwise it would be parallel to a row that it already contains).  \n   Consequently, in order to cover the \\(P_k\\) points of (1) we need at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{P_k}{2k}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k(2k+1)}{2k}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =k+1\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines that are **not** horizontal.  \n   Since we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines in total, the number of non‑horizontal lines is at most \\(n\\).  \n   If we had more than \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, then the number of non‑horizontal lines would be at least  \n\n   \\[\n   k+\\bigl(n-k\\bigr)=n\\ge 2k+1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. at least \\(2k+1\\) non‑horizontal lines.  \n   But (2) tells us that covering the points of the top \\(2k\\) rows already forces **\\(k+1\\)** non‑horizontal lines; the remaining \\(k\\) non‑horizontal lines would have to be horizontal, which is impossible because a horizontal line can intersect at most one of those \\(2k\\) rows.  \n   Hence we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\le n-1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (3) is a necessary condition for the existence of a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   m=n-2k\\qquad(\\;m\\ge 1\\;).\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Take the \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,m). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of the rows \\(b=1,\\dots ,m\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=x+(m+i-1). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of each \\(L_i\\) is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the \\(L_i\\) are sunny and they are distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines (4)–(5).*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\le m\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n   *If \\(b>m\\)*, write \\(b=m+t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le 2k\\).  \n   The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le n+1-b=n+1-(m+t)=2k+1-t . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the points of the row \\(b\\) that have to be covered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,m+t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le 2k+1-t\\}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(t\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   i_0=\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{t}{2}\\bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   For every integer \\(i\\) with \\(i_0\\le i\\le k\\) the point of \\(L_i\\) that lies in the row \\(b=m+t\\) is obtained by solving \\(y=m+t\\) in (5):\n\n   \\[\n   m+t = x+(m+i-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x = t-i+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the point of \\(L_i\\) in that row is \\((t-i+1,m+t)\\).  \n   Because \\(i\\ge i_0\\),\n\n   \\[\n   1\\le t-i+1\\le 2k+1-t ,\n   \\]\n\n   the first inequality follows from \\(i\\le t\\) (which holds for all \\(i\\ge i_0\\) because \\(i_0\\le t\\)), and the second one is equivalent to \\(i\\ge\\lceil t/2\\rceil\\).  \n   Consequently each integer \\(a\\) satisfying (6) appears exactly once as \\(a=t-i+1\\) for a suitable \\(i\\) with \\(i_0\\le i\\le k\\).  \n   Hence the whole set (7) is covered by the sunny lines \\(L_{i_0},L_{i_0+1},\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   In both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (4)–(5).  \n   The family consists of \\(m\\) horizontal lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(n=m+k\\) distinct lines, and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) a required configuration exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (3) shows that no configuration can contain more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, and the explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   can indeed be realised.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the set displayed in the boxed answer. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
