y  \n\n     \\[\n     L_i:\\;y = x + (m+i-1).\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n   The family consists of exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines; the \\(k\\) lines in (1) have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and are therefore sunny.\n\n   **Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\le m\\).*  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n   *If \\(b>m\\).*  \n   Write \\(b=m+t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).  \n   The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le n+1-b=n+1-(m+t)=k+1-t .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the points that have to be covered in the row \\(y=m+t\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,m+t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k+1-t\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(t\\) and for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(t\\le i\\le k\\) the line \\(L_i\\) meets the row \\(y=m+t\\) at the point obtained by solving \\(y=m+t\\) in (1):\n\n   \\[\n   m+t = x+(m+i-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x = t-i+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(i\\ge t\\), the right\u2011hand side of (4) satisfies \\(x\\le 0\\); therefore we **do not** obtain points of the form (3) from such \\(i\\).  \n   Instead we take the indices \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k+1-t\\).  \n   For any such \\(i\\) we have\n\n   \\[\n   x = t-i+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad 1\\le x\\le k+1-t .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, the lower inequality \\(x\\ge1\\) is equivalent to \\(i\\le t\\), which holds because \\(i\\le k+1-t\\le t\\) (the latter follows from \\(t\\le k\\)).  \n   The upper inequality \\(x\\le k+1-t\\) is equivalent to \\(i\\ge1\\), which is true.  \n   Consequently, when \\(i\\) runs through the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k+1-t\\}\\) the points \\((x,m+t)\\) given by (4) run exactly through the whole set (3).  \n\n   Thus every point of the row \\(y=m+t\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{k+1-t}\\).  \n\n   Combining the two cases, every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\