            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous construction for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=0\\), \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable.\n* No value \\(k>n\\) is possible (obviously, because we have only \\(n\\) lines).\n\nThus the set of admissible values is known to contain \\(\\{0,1,n\\}\\) and to be contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThe remaining cases \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) remain open in the present write‑up.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   Since we are allowed to use only \\(n\\) distinct lines, we must have \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n   \\]\n   All of them have slope \\(0\\) (hence are not sunny) and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the line\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y=x+n-1 .\n   \\]\n   The line \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(1\\) (so it is sunny) and passes through the unique point of \\(T_n\\) that is not already covered by the horizontal lines, namely \\((1,n)\\). Hence exactly one line is sunny.\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=n\\).**  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; \\text{through }(1,i)\\text{ and }(i,1).\n   \\]\n   Its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{i-1}{i-1}=-1\\) when \\(i\\ge2\\), which is *not* sunny; however we can modify the construction as follows.\n   \n   Choose a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) that has no fixed point (a derangement, which exists for every \\(n\\ge2\\)).  \n   For each \\(i\\) set\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;\\text{the line through }(1,i)\\text{ and }(\\sigma(i),1).\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq1\\), the slope of \\(L_i\\) equals\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{1-i}{\\sigma(i)-1},\n   \\]\n   which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently **all** the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Moreover, for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(\\sigma(b)=a\\) for a unique \\(b\\) (since \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection), and then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\). Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The three explicit constructions show that the values \\(k=0\\), \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are realizable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Because we cannot use more than \\(n\\) lines, no larger \\(k\\) is possible.  \n   The existence (or non‑existence) of configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for the intermediate values \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) remains unresolved in this answer. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
