            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConsider the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the lines (3).  \nThe set of points that are **not** covered by (3) is\n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n-k}H_j\n   =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\;b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(b' = b-(n-k)\\) and \\(a'=a\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na'+b'\\le k+1,\\qquad a',b'\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points are precisely a translate of  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, to obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines it suffices to exhibit **\\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_k\\)**.  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines will be the horizontals (3).  \nThe case \\(k=0\\) is realised by taking the \\(n\\) horizontals alone; the case \\(k=n\\) corresponds to taking no horizontals at all.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Covering the triangle \\(T_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nWe prove the following.\n\n> **Lemma 1.** For every integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) there exist exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains the whole set \\(T_k\\) defined in (4).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\text{ the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,k+2-i).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i=1\\) we set  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\; y = x+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\n*Sunnyness.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_i\\;(i\\ge 2)\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{k+2-i-1}{1-i}= -\\frac{k+1-i}{\\,i-1\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le i-1\\le k\\) and \\(0\\le k+1-i\\le k\\), the fraction \\(-\\dfrac{k+1-i}{i-1}\\) is never \\(0\\), never infinite, and never \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\;(i\\ge 2)\\) is sunny.  \nThe line \\(L_1\\) has slope \\(1\\), also sunny.  Thus we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\); i.e. \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\ni:=a+1\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le k+1-a = k+2-i .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\((i,1)=(a+1,1)\\) and \\((1,k+2-i)\\) are the two defining points of the line \\(L_i\\) (for \\(i\\ge 2\\); when \\(i=1\\) the line is \\(L_1\\) given by (6)).  \nEquation (5) (or (6) when \\(i=1\\)) can be written as  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y-1}{x-i}= \\frac{k+2-i-1}{1-i}= -\\frac{k+1-i}{i-1}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) and using \\(i=a+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y-1}{a-(a+1)} = -\\frac{k+1-(a+1)}{a}= -\\frac{k-a}{a}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(-a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = k-a .\n\\]\n\nHence the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (8) exactly when \\(b = k-a+1 = k+2-i\\).  \nIf equality holds, \\((a,b)\\) is one of the two defining points of \\(L_i\\) and therefore lies on \\(L_i\\).  \n\nIf \\(b<k+2-i\\) (the strict inequality in (7)), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the **segment** joining \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,k+2-i)\\); any line contains the whole segment determined by its two defining points, so \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to \\(L_i\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to the line \\(L_{a+1}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nT_k\\subseteq \\bigcup_{i=1}^{k} L_i .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); all of them are non‑sunny, so exactly \\(0\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n* If \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) we proceed as follows.  \n  – Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (3); they are non‑sunny.  \n  – Apply Lemma 1 to the integer \\(k\\) and obtain the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \n  – Translate the \\(k\\) lines of Lemma 1 upward by \\(n-k\\) units (replace each equation \\(y = m x + c\\) by \\(y = m x + c + (n-k)\\)).  Translation does not change the slope, hence the lines remain sunny and stay distinct.  \n\n  The translated sunny lines cover exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate exceeds \\(n-k\\); the horizontals cover the remaining points.  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nAny configuration uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Therefore no integer \\(k>n\\) is admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer  \n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\) inclusive:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
