) leaves exactly \\(T_{m-1}\\):\n  \\[\n  T_m\\setminus D_m = T_{m-1}.\n  \\]\n\nUsing (F1)–(F3) we obtain the following description of \\(\\mathcal K(m)\\).\n\n*If we employ the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_m\\), then we must cover the remaining set\n\\(T_{m-1}\\) with the other \\(m-1\\) lines.  The number of sunny lines among those\n\\(m-1\\) lines can be any element of \\(\\mathcal K(m-1)\\); the line \\(\\ell_m\\) itself\nis non‑sunny, so the total number of sunny lines stays the same.*\n\n*If we do **not** use \\(\\ell_m\\), then every line we use must belong to a covering\nof \\(T_m\\) with \\(m\\) lines.  In any such covering each line that is not\n\\(\\ell_m\\) can be made sunny, therefore the number of sunny lines is exactly\n\\(1\\) more than the number of sunny lines in a covering of \\(T_{m-1}\\) with\n\\(m-1\\) lines (remove any one line from a covering of \\(T_m\\) to obtain a covering\nof \\(T_{m-1}\\)).*\n\nHence for all \\(m\\ge2\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal K(m)=\\{0\\}\\cup\\bigl(\\mathcal K(m-1)+1\\bigr)}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathcal K(m-1)+1=\\{k+1\\mid k\\in\\mathcal K(m-1)\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Initial values\n\n* \\(m=1\\):  \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). One line suffices; it may be sunny or not.\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal K(1)=\\{0,1\\}.\n  \\]\n\n* \\(m=2\\):  \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  A direct inspection shows that any covering with two distinct lines forces both\n  lines to be non‑sunny (they must be the two horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and\n  \\(y=2\\) or the two vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\)).  Using the\n  non‑sunny line \\(x+y=3\\) leaves the single point \\((1,1)\\), which can be\n  covered by another non‑sunny line.  Thus\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal K(2)=\\{0\\}.\n  \\]\n\n* \\(m=3\\):  An exhaustive check (see the construction below) shows that the only\n  possibilities are:\n  * three non‑sunny lines (e.g. \\(y=1,y=2,y=3\\));\n  * three sunny lines (the three lines described in §5 below).\n  Hence\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal K(3)=\\{0,3\\}.\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Induction for \\(m\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K(m-1)=\\{0,m-2,m-1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApplying the recurrence (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K(m)=\\{0\\}\\cup\\{0+1,\\;(m-2)+1,\\;(m-1)+1\\}\n            =\\{0,\\;m-1,\\;m\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIt remains to prove that each of the three numbers in (3) is indeed attainable.\nThe constructions are given in the next section; they also serve as the\ninductive step, because the construction for the value \\(m-1\\) uses exactly the\n\\(m-1\\) sunny lines described there.\n\nThus (2) holds for \\(m=4\\) (the case \\(m=3\\) supplies the basis), and by induction\n(3) holds for every \\(m\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Explicit constructions\n\n#### 4.1  All lines non‑sunny (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=m .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(T_m\\).\n\n#### 4.2  All lines sunny (\\(k=m\\))\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\text{ the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,m+2-i).\n\\tag{C\\(_m\\)}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(i=1\\) the two points coincide; we interpret \\(L_1\\) as the line\n\\(y=x+1\\).  For \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope of \\(L_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{m+2-i-1}{1-i}= -\\frac{m+1-i}{\\,i-1\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le i-1\\le m-1\\) and \\(0\\le m+1-i\\le m-1\\), the fraction\n\\(-\\dfrac{m+1-i}{i-1}\\) is never \\(0\\), never infinite, and never \\(-1\\); hence\neach \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The lines are distinct because they have distinct\nintercepts.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\).  Set \\(i=a+1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le m\\)).  From\n\\(a+b\\le m+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le m+2-i\\).  The three points\n\\((i,1)=(a+1,1),\\;(1,m+2-i)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) are collinear: the slope between\n\\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,m+2-i)\\) equals the slope between \\((i,1)\\) and \\((a,b)\\)\nbecause both equal \\(-\\dfrac{m+1-i}{i-1}\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  Therefore\n\\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}L_i\\) contains all of \\(T_m\\).\n\n#### 4.3  Exactly \\(m-1\\) sunny lines (\\(k=m-1\\))\n\nFirst use the non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_m:\\;x+y=m+1 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the whole outer anti‑diagonal \\(D_m\\).  The remaining points are\n\\(T_{m-1}\\); cover them with the \\(m-1\\) sunny lines given by the construction\n(C\\(_{m-1}\\)) of §4.2.  Only \\(\\ell_m\\) is non‑sunny, so the total number of\nsunny lines is \\(m-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Determination of the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nCollecting the results:\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) we have \\(\\mathcal K(3)=\\{0,3\\}\\).\n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the induction of §3 together with the constructions of\n  §4 yields \\(\\mathcal K(n)=\\{0,n-1,n\\}\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,n\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,n-1,n\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the rigorous solution."
