n at most one point.  \n*Verdict:* **Correct.**\n\n---\n\n**Quote (F2):**  \n> “The whole set \\(D_m\\) lies on the single non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_m:\\;x+y=m+1\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBy definition, \\(\\ell_m\\) is exactly the line containing all points with \\(a+b=m+1\\). Its slope is \\(-1\\), so it is non‑sunny.  \n*Verdict:* **Correct.**\n\n---\n\n**Quote (F3):**  \n> “Removing \\(D_m\\) from \\(T_m\\) leaves exactly \\(T_{m-1}\\): \\(T_m\\setminus D_m = T_{m-1}\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\\(T_m\\) consists of points with \\(a+b\\le m+1\\); removing those with equality leaves points with \\(a+b\\le m\\), which is precisely \\(T_{m-1}\\).  \n*Verdict:* **Correct.**\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Claimed Recurrence  \n\n**Quote:**  \n> “Hence for all \\(m\\ge2\\)  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\mathcal K(m)=\\{0\\}\\cup\\bigl(\\mathcal K(m-1)+1\\bigr)}\\tag{1}\n> \\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe derivation of (1) is based on two cases:\n\n1. **Using \\(\\ell_m\\).**  \n   If \\(\\ell_m\\) is among the \\(m\\) lines, the remaining \\(m-1\\) lines must cover \\(T_{m-1}\\). The number of sunny lines among those \\(m-1\\) lines can be any element of \\(\\mathcal K(m-1)\\); since \\(\\ell_m\\) is non‑sunny, the total number of sunny lines equals the same element of \\(\\mathcal K(m-1)\\). Hence **\\(\\mathcal K(m)\\) must contain \\(\\mathcal K(m-1)\\).** The formula (1) omits this entire set (except possibly the element \\(0\\)), which is a logical omission.\n\n2. **Not using \\(\\ell_m\\).**  \n   The solution claims: “every line that is not \\(\\ell_m\\) can be made sunny, therefore the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(1\\) more than the number of sunny lines in a covering of \\(T_{m-1}\\) with \\(m-1\\) lines.”  \n   This claim is false. A line that does **not** equal \\(\\ell_m\\) may still be forced to be non‑sunny (e.g. a vertical line \\(x=1\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) can be needed to cover points of \\(T_{m-1}\\)). There is no justification that we can always replace a non‑