g of \\(T_{m-1}\\) with \\(m-1\\) lines; by the induction hypothesis the remaining \\(m-1\\) lines may have any number of sunny lines from \\(S(m-1)\\). Adding back the removed line (which is sunny) gives the possibilities \\(1\\) and \\(m\\) sunny lines for \\(T_m\\).  \n   Collecting the four possibilities yields (L1). \u220e\n\n3. **From Lemma\u202f1 to the original problem.**  \n   The required set of points for the given integer \\(n\\) is exactly \\(T_n\\).  \n   *If \\(n=3\\):* Lemma\u202f1 gives \\(S(3)=\\{0,3\\}\\); the value \\(k=1\\) is impossible (a short direct verification).  \n   *If \\(n\\ge4\\):* Lemma\u202f1 gives \\(S(n)=\\{0,1,n-1,n\\}\\).  \n\n4. **Explicit constructions** (showing that every value in the sets above is indeed attainable).  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*\u2003Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\). All are non\u2011sunny and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*\u2003For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define a line \\(L_i\\) as follows  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\text{the line through }(1,n)\\text{ and }(2,1), & i=1,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\text{the line through }(i,n+1-i)\\text{ and }(1,1), & 2\\le i\\le n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\text{the line through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(2,2), & i=n\\;(n\\ge4),\\\\[2mm]\n   \\text{the line through }(3,1)\\text{ and }(1,2), & i=n=3 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) the slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n-i}{i-1}\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   For \\(i=1\\) the slope is \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) (since \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \n   For \\(i=n\\) (when \\(n\\ge4\\)) the slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{2-n}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   All \\(L_i\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.  \n   A direct computation shows that a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_{a+1}\\); consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\) (for \\(n\\ge4\\)).*\u2003Take the non\u2011sunny