b Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\\qquad m\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nFor a fixed \\(m\\) denote  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K(m)=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\\exists\\;m\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_m,\n\\text{ exactly }k\\text{ of them sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge2\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal K(m)=\\{0,\\,1,\\,m-1,\\,m\\}} .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n**Base \\(m=2\\).**  \n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nA covering with two lines is either  \n\n* the two horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2\\) (both non\u2011sunny), giving \\(k=0\\); or  \n* the two lines \\(x+y=3\\) (non\u2011sunny) and \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny), again \\(k=0\\);  \n\nor the two lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) (both non\u2011sunny).  \nThe only way to obtain a sunny line is to use the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (horizontal) \u2013 not sunny \u2013 or through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) (vertical) \u2013 not sunny.  \nHence the only attainable numbers are \\(0\\) and \\(2\\). Since \\(2=m\\), (1) holds for \\(m=2\\).\n\n**Induction step.** Assume (1) holds for some \\(m-1\\ge2\\).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nD_m=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+1\\}\n\\]\n\n(the outer anti\u2011diagonal). Any two distinct points of \\(D_m\\) have slope \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(D_m\\).\n\nConsider a covering of \\(T_m\\) by \\(m\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 the line \\(\\ell_m:x+y=m+1\\) is used.*  \n\\(\\ell_m\\) is non\u2011sunny and covers the whole set \\(D_m\\).  \nThe remaining points are exactly \\(T_{m-1}\\); they must be covered by the other \\(m-1\\) lines.  \nBy the induction hypothesis the number of sunny lines among those \\(m-1\\) lines can be any element of \\(\\mathcal K(m-1)=\\{0,1,m-2,m-1\\}\\).  \nAdding the non\u2011sunny \\(\\ell_m\\) leaves the same numbers for the whole covering, i.e. we obtain the possibilities  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,m-1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\(\\ell_m\\) is **not** used.*  \nSince each sunny line can meet at most one point of \\(D_m\\), the \\(m\\) lines must contain **exactly one** point of \\(D_m\\) each; therefore every line is forced to be sunny (otherwise a non\u2011sunny line would be parallel to \\(\\ell_m\\) and could not meet its own point of \\(D_m\\)).  \nThus \\(k=m\\).\n\nCombining the two cases yields (1). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Application to the original problem\n\nFor the given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of required points is precisely \\(T_n\\).  \nHence the admissible numbers of sunny lines are the elements of \\(\\mathcal K(n)\\).\n\n*If \\(n=3\\).*  \nLemma\u202f1 gives \\(\\mathcal K(3)=\\{0,3\\}\\).  \nA direct inspection shows that \\(k=1\\) cannot occur (the three points of the outer anti\u2011diagonal would need three distinct non\u2011parallel lines, forcing all three lines to be sunny).  \n\n*If \\(n\\ge4\\).*  \nLemma\u202f1 gives \\(\\mathcal K(n)=\\{0,1,n-1,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions showing attainability\n\nWe present explicit families of lines for each admissible value.\n\n1. **\\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n   \\]\n\n   are all non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n2. **\\(k=n\\).**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define a line \\(L_i\\) as follows  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\text{through }(1,n)\\text{ and }(2,1), & i=1,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\text{through }(i,n+1-i)\\text{ and }(1,1), & 2\\le i\\le n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(2,2), & i=n\\;(n\\ge4),\\\\[2mm]\n   \\text{through }(3,1)\\text{ and }(1,2), & i=n=3 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   *Slopes.*  \n   \u2013 For \\(i=1\\) the slope is \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   \u2013 For \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) the slope equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n-i}{i-1}\\), never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   \u2013 For \\(i=n\\) (when \\(n\\ge4\\)) the slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{2-n}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   \u2013 For \\(n=3\\) the slope of the last line is \\(-\\tfrac12\\).  \n\n   Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny. The slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Set \\(i=a+1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  \n   One checks that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); consequently each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the \\(L_i\\). Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover the whole set.\n\n3. **\\(k=n-1\\) (for \\(n\\ge4\\)).**  \n   Use the non\u2011sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_n:\\;x+y=n+1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which covers the outer anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_n\\).  \n   The remaining points form \\(T_{n-1}\\); cover them with the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines described in item\u202f2 for the integer \\(n-1\\). Exactly one line (\\(\\ell_n\\)) is non\u2011sunny, so the total number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\).\n\n4. **\\(k=1\\) (for \\(n\\ge4\\)).**  \n   Again take \\(\\ell_n:x+y=n+1\\).  \n   Inside \\(T_{n-1}\\) keep the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   all non\u2011sunny.  \n   The only point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) not yet covered is \\((1,n-1)\\).  \n   Cover it with the sunny line through \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); its slope is \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Altogether we have one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=1\\).\n\nThus every integer listed in the statement is indeed realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of all other values\n\nLet a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*If the