            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\\qquad m\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nand denote by  \n\n\\[\nD_m=\\{(a,b)\\in T_m\\mid a+b=m+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,m),(2,m-1),\\dots ,(m,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nthe outer anti‑diagonal of the triangle \\(T_m\\).\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Two elementary facts\n\n*Fact 1.*  Any two distinct points of \\(D_m\\) have slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(D_m\\).\n\n*Fact 2.*  The line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_m:\\;x+y=m+1\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(-1\\) and contains **all** points of \\(D_m\\).  \nIt is the only line that can meet more than one point of \\(D_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Recursive description of the possible numbers of sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS(m)=\\{k\\ge 0\\mid\\exists\\;m\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_m\n      \\text{ with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe analyse the two mutually exclusive possibilities concerning the line \\(\\ell_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The line \\(\\