f \\(T_{n-1}\\) not yet covered is \\((1,n-1)\\); cover it with the sunny line through \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Altogether we have one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\) (all but one line sunny, \\(n\\ge 4\\)).*  \n   Again start with \\(\\ell_n\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   By the induction hypothesis for \\(n-1\\) we can cover \\(T_{n-1}\\) with \\(n-1\\) lines that are **all sunny** (the case \\(k=n-1\\) for size \\(n-1\\) is exactly the construction described in the next bullet).  \n   Adding \\(\\ell_n\\) gives a covering of \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\) for \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: (1,3)\\text{–}(2,1) \\quad(\\text{slope }-2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: (2,2)\\text{–}(1,1) \\quad(\\text{slope } 1),\\\\\n   L_3 &: (3,1)\\text{–}(1,2) \\quad(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, sunny and together contain all six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of any other value of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *For \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The exhaustive check of all possibilities shows that the only attainable numbers are those listed in (4).\n\n   *For \\(n\\ge 4\\).*  \n   Let a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines contain \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   – If the line \\(\\ell_n\\) is used, then the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines cover \\(T_{n-1}\\); by the induction hypothesis the number of sunny lines among them belongs to \\(\\{0,1,n-2\\}\\). Hence \\(k\\in\\{0,1,n-2\\}\\).\n\n   – If \\(\\ell_n\\) is **not** used, we are in the situation described in §3.2: each line meets exactly one point of \\(D_n\\).  \n     As proved there, the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0\\) (all lines horizontal) and \\(1\\) (exactly one line is sunny, the other \\(n-1\\) being horizontal or vertical). Thus \\(k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n   Collecting the two cases gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,n-2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   But by the construction of §5 the value \\(n-2\\) is realised **only** when we start from a covering of \\(T_{n-1}\\) with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n   The induction hypothesis tells us that for \\(n-1\\ge 4\\) the maximal number of sunny lines is \\((n-1)-1=n-2\\); therefore the value \\(n-2\\) indeed occurs, and it is precisely the case \\(k=n-1\\) for the original size \\(n\\).  \n   No other integer can appear.\n\n   Consequently, for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\\qquad m\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nand denote by  \n\n\\[\nD_m=\\{(a,b)\\in T_m\\mid a+b=m+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,m),(2,m-1),\\dots ,(m,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nthe outer anti‑diagonal of the triangle \\(T_m\\).\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Two elementary facts\n\n*Fact 1.*  Any two distinct points of \\(D_m\\) have slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(D_m\\).\n\n*Fact 2.*  The line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_m:\\;x+y=m+1\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(-1\\) and contains **all** points of \\(D_m\\).  \nIt is the only line that can meet more than one point of \\(D_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Recursive description of the possible numbers of sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS(m)=\\{k\\ge 0\\mid\\exists\\;m\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_m\n      \\text{ with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe analyse the two mutually exclusive possibilities concerning the line \\(\\ell_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The line \\(\\ell_m\\) is used  \n\nIf \\(\\ell_m\\) belongs to the covering, it is non‑sunny and covers the whole set \\(D_m\\).  \nThe remaining \\(m-1\\) lines must cover the set  \n\n\\[\nT_m\\setminus D_m = T_{m-1}.\n\\]\n\nHence, if \\(\\ell_m\\) is used, the number of sunny lines equals the number of sunny lines in a covering of \\(T_{m-1}\\) with \\(m-1\\) lines; i.e.\n\n\\[\nS(m)\\supseteq S(m-1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The line \\(\\ell_m\\) is **not** used  \n\nThen each of the \\(m\\) lines contains exactly one point of \\(D_m\\) (Fact 1).  \nA line that is not sunny can only be horizontal (\\(y=b\\)) or vertical (\\(x=a\\)), because a line of slope \\(-1\\) would be \\(\\ell_m\\).\n\nA horizontal line \\(y=b\\) meeting the point \\((a,b)\\in D_m\\) also contains every point \\((x,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\nx+b\\le m+1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x\\le a .\n\\]\n\nThus it covers the whole **row** \\(\\{y=b\\}\\) inside the triangle, up to the column of its D‑point.  \nAnalogously a vertical line \\(x=a\\) covers the whole **column** \\(\\{x=a\\}\\) up to the row of its D‑point.\n\nInside \\(T_m\\) there are exactly \\(m-1\\) rows \\(\\{y=2,\\dots ,y=m\\}\\) and \\(m-1\\) columns \\(\\{x=2,\\dots ,x=m\\}\\); each of them is disjoint from the others.  \nA horizontal line can cover at most one of those rows, and a vertical line at most one of those columns.  \nConsequently, to cover all interior points of \\(T_m\\) we need at least \\(m-1\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nIf at least two of the \\(m\\) lines are sunny, then the number of non‑sunny lines is at most \\(m-2\\), which is insufficient to cover the interior.  \nTherefore, **without using \\(\\ell_m\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).**  \n\nHence, for every \\(m\\ge 4\\),\n\n\\[\nS(m)=\\{0,1\\}\\cup S(m-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of the sets \\(S(m)\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The case \\(m=3\\)\n\nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nA direct inspection yields three feasible coverings:\n\n* three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\) – \\(k=0\\);\n* the three lines listed in §5 (all sunny) – \\(k=3\\);\n* the covering consisting of \\(\\ell_3:x+y=4\\) together with the sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) – \\(k=1\\).\n\nNo covering with exactly two sunny lines exists (any line through two points of \\(T_3\\) is non‑sunny).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nS(3)=\\{0,1,3\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Induction for \\(m\\ge 4\\)\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge 4\\)\n\n\\[\nS(m-1)=\\{0,1,m-2\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nApplying (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nS(m)=\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{0,1,m-2\\}\n     =\\{0,1,m-1\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus (6) holds for every \\(m\\ge 4\\).  \nTogether with (4) we have completely described the sets \\(S(m)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Explicit constructions for the attainable values\n\nBelow we give concrete families of lines for each admissible value of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny)\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n\\]\n\nare distinct, non‑sunny and clearly contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line, \\(n\\ge 4\\))\n\nTake the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_n:x+y=n+1\\) to cover the outer anti‑diagonal \\(D_n\\).  \nInside \\(T_{n-1}\\) keep the \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\).  \nThe only point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) still uncovered is \\((1,n-1)\\); cover it with the sunny line through \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nExactly one line