 interior points (it can meet at most one point of a given row and at most one point of a given column; a third interior point would force the slope to be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consequently, if only the line \\(\\ell_m\\) is non‑sunny, the remaining \\(m-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2(m-1)\\) interior points, which is insufficient for \\(m\\ge5\\).  \n   Therefore, when \\(\\ell_m\\) is not used, the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0\\) (all lines horizontal) and \\(1\\) (exactly one sunny line, the other \\(m-1\\) lines horizontal). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   S(m)=\\{0,1\\}\\cup S(m-1)\\qquad(m\\ge5). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Base cases.**  \n   *\\(m=3\\).* A direct inspection of the six points of \\(T_3\\) shows  \n\n   \\[\n   S(3)=\\{0,1,3\\}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(m=4\\).* Using the construction described in §5 below one obtains  \n\n   \\[\n   S(4)=\\{0,1,3\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Induction.**  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge5\\) we already know  \n\n   \\[\n   S(m-1)=\\{0,1,m-3\\}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Applying (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   S(m)=\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{0,1,m-3\\}=\\{0,1,m-2\\}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus (6) holds for every \\(m\\ge5\\). Together with (3) and (4) we have determined all sets \\(S(m)\\).\n\n6. **Explicit constructions for the attainable values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are distinct, non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the sunny line through the two points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\).  \n   Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=n-2\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\).*  \n   – Take the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_n:x+y=n+1\\).  \n   – Take the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   – For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) draw the sunny line of slope \\(1\\) through the points  \n     \\[\n     \\bigl(j+1,\\;n-j\\bigr)\\in D_n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\bigl(j,\\;n-1-j\\bigr)\\in T_{n-1}.\n     \\]\n     The line has equation \\(y=n-1-x\\) and therefore contains exactly the interior point \\((j,n-1-j)\\).  \n   The \\(n\\) lines are distinct; the only non‑sunny ones are \\(\\ell_n\\) and \\(y=1\\); all the other \\(n-2\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the lines, so \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3,4\\).*  \n   The three lines listed below are sunny and together contain all points of \\(T_n\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   n=3:&\\quad (1,3)-(2,1),\\;(2,2)-(1,1),\\;(3,1)-(1,2);\\\\\n   n=4:&\\quad (1,3)-(2,1),\\;(2,2)-(1,1),\\;(3,1)-(1,2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Impossibility of any other value of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *For \\(n\\ge5\\).*  \n   Suppose a covering of \\(T_n\\) uses \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   – By Fact 1 each sunny line contains at most one point of \\(D_n\\); the remaining interior points are at most two per sunny line, i.e. at most \\(2s\\) interior points.  \n   – A non‑sunny line can be horizontal or vertical; such a line can cover at most \\(n-1\\) interior points, and two different non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-1)+(n-2)=2n-3\\) distinct interior points (they must lie in different rows or columns).  \n   Hence the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2s+(2n-3).\n   \\]\n\n   The interior of \\(T_n\\) consists of \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points.  \n   The inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   2s+(2n-3)\\ge\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   forces  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\ge n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\(s\\) can be only \\(0,1\\) or \\(n-2\\).  \n   The constructions of §5 show that each of these three values is indeed attainable; no other value can occur.\n\n   *For \\(n=3,4\\).*  \n   Direct inspection (or the constructions above) shows that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0,1,3\\). No covering with exactly two sunny lines exists because, as argued above, a sunny line can meet at most two interior points, and with two sunny lines at most four interior points would be covered, whereas \\(T_3\\) and \\(T_4\\) contain six interior points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\\qquad \nD_m=\\{(a,b)\\in T_m\\mid a+b=m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set  \n\n\\[\nS(n)=\\{k\\ge0\\mid\\exists\\;n\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_n\n          \\text{ with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary observations  \n\n*Any two points of \\(D_m\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(D_m\\).*  \n*The line \\(\\ell_m:x+y=m+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) contains all points of \\(D_m\\); it is the only line that can meet more than one point of \\(D_m\\).*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Recurrence for \\(S(m)\\)\n\nConsider a covering of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Case A – the line \\(\\ell_m\\) is used.*  \n\\(\\ell_m\\) is non‑sunny and covers the whole set \\(D_m\\).  \nThe remaining \\(m-1\\) lines must cover \\(T_{m-1}\\); consequently the number of sunny lines among them belongs to \\(S(m-1)\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nS(m)\\supseteq S(m-1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Case B – the line \\(\\ell_m\\) is **not** used.*  \nThen each of the \\(m\\) lines contains exactly one point of \\(D_m\\).  \nA non‑sunny line can be only horizontal (\\(y=b\\)) or vertical (\\(x=a\\)).  \nA horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains all points \\((x,b)\\) with \\(x\\le m+1-b\\); a vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains all points \\((a,y)\\) with \\(y\\le m+1-a\\).  \nThus a non‑sunny line can cover at most one whole row or one whole column of the interior set  \n\n\\[\nI_m:=T_m\\setminus D_m=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le m\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(I_m\\): if it contained three, two of them would lie in the same row or the same column, forcing the slope to be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  \n\nConsequently, if only \\(\\ell_m\\) is non‑sunny, the \\(m-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2(m-1)\\) points of \\(I_m\\).  \nSince \\(|I_m|=\\dfrac{(m-1)m}{2}\\), the inequality  \n\n\\[\n2(m-1)\\ge\\frac{(m-1)m}{2}\n\\]\n\nholds only for \\(m\\le4\\).  \nHence for \\(m\\ge5\\) the situation “\\(\\ell_m\\) not used’’ forces **all** non‑sunny lines to be horizontal (or vertical).  \nWith at most one horizontal line we obtain exactly the two possibilities\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{(all lines horizontal)}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k=1\\quad\\text{(one sunny line, the others horizontal)}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, for every \\(m\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\nS(m)=\\{0,1\\}\\cup S(m-1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Small values  \n\n*For \\(m=3\\)* a direct check of the six points of \\(T_3\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nS(3)=\\{0,1,3\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*For \\(m=4\\)* the following three sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_4\\):\n\n\\[\n(1,3)-(2,1),\\qquad (2,2)-(1,1),\\qquad (3,1)-(1,2),\n\\]\n\nso \\(3\\in S(4)\\). Together with the obvious constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nS(4)=\\{0,1,3\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Induction for \\(m\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\nS(m-1)=\\{0,1,m-3\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nApplying (2) gives  \n\n\\[\nS(m)=\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{0,1,m-3\\}=\\{0,1,m-2\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus (6) holds for every \\(m\\ge5\\).  \nCombining (3), (4) and (6) we have completely determined the sets \\(S(m)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Explicit coverings for the attainable values  \n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are distinct, non‑sunny and contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nAdd the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\).  \nExactly one line is sunny.\n\n*\\(k=n-2\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\).*  \n1. The non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_n:x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n2. The horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n3. For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) the sunny line of slope \\(1\\) through the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(j+1,\\;n-j)\\in D_n,\\qquad Q_j=(j,\\;n-1-j)\\in T_{n-1}.\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(P_jQ_j\\) has equation \\(y=n-1-x\\) and therefore contains exactly the interior point \\(Q_j\\).  \n\n   The \\(n\\) lines are distinct; the only non‑sunny ones are \\(\\ell_n\\) and \\(y=1\\); the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the lines, so \\(k=n-2\\) is realised.\n\n*\\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3,4\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines displayed in §4 (for \\(n=3\\) and for \\(n=4\\)) cover all points of the corresponding triangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  No other values of \\(k\\) are possible  \n\nLet a covering of \\(T_n\\) contain \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Each sunny line contains at most two interior points* (Section 2).  \nHence the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2s\\) points of the interior set  \n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |I_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny line is horizontal or vertical; such a line can cover at most \\(n-1\\) interior points, and two different non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-1)+(n-2)=2n-3\\) distinct interior points (they must lie in different rows or columns).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n2s+(2n-3)\\ge\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) inequality (7) forces \\(s\\ge n-2\\).  \nThus the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0,1\\) or \\(n-2\\).  \nThe constructions of §5 show that each of these three numbers is attainable; therefore no other value of \\(k\\) can occur when \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\nFor \\(n=3,4\\) a direct inspection of the six (resp. ten) points of \\(T_n\\) shows that the only attainable numbers are \\(0,1,3\\). In particular, a covering with exactly two sunny lines would leave at most four interior points covered (each sunny line contributes at most two), while \\(T_3\\) and \\(T_4\\) contain six interior points, a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Hence the complete answer is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,n-2\\}, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
