ver at most one whole row or one whole column of the interior set  \n\n\\[\nI_m:=T_m\\setminus D_m=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le m\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(I_m\\): if it contained three, two of them would lie in the same row or the same column, forcing the slope to be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  \n\nConsequently, if only \\(\\ell_m\\) is non\u2011sunny, the \\(m-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2(m-1)\\) points of \\(I_m\\).  \nSince \\(|I_m|=\\dfrac{(m-1)m}{2}\\), the inequality  \n\n\\[\n2(m-1)\\ge\\frac{(m-1)m}{2}\n\\]\n\nholds only for \\(m\\le4\\).  \nHence for \\(m\\ge5\\) the situation \u201c\\(\\ell_m\\) not used\u2019\u2019 forces **all** non\u2011sunny lines to be horizontal (or vertical).  \nWith at most one horizontal line we obtain exactly the two possibilities\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{(all lines horizontal)}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k=1\\quad\\text{(one sunny line, the others horizontal)}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, for every \\(m\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\nS(m)=\\{0,1\\}\\cup S(m-1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Small values  \n\n*For \\(m=3\\)* a direct check of the six points of \\(T_3\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nS(3)=\\{0,1,3\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*For \\(m=4\\)* the following three sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_4\\):\n\n\\[\n(1,3)-(2,1),\\qquad (2,2)-(1,1),\\qquad (3,1)-(1,2),\n\\]\n\nso \\(3\\in S(4)\\). Together with the obvious constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nS(4)=\\{0,1,3\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Induction for \\(m\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\nS(m-1)=\\{0,1,m-3\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nApplying (2) gives  \n\n\\[\nS(m)=\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{0,1,m-3\\}=\\{0,1,m-2\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus (6) holds for every \\(m\\ge5\\).  \nCombining (3), (4) and (6) we have completely determined the sets \\(S(m)\\).\n\